The cleanest way to run this on your Helpdesk is to wrap the PowerShell GUI as a Windows .exe, so Helpdesk gets something like:

LAPS Password Lookup.exe

Double-click it, GUI opens, no PowerShell window, no script execution-policy hassle for the technician.

Use PS2EXE. On the machine you use to build it, open an elevated powershell console:

Install-Module ps2exe -Scope CurrentUser

Then:

Invoke-PS2EXE `
    -InputFile ".\LAPS-Modern.ps1" `
    -OutputFile ".\LAPS-Password-Lookup.exe" `
    -NoConsole `
    -x64 `
    -Title "LAPS Password Lookup" `
    -Product "Helpdesk Tools" `
    -Company "<insert company name here>" `
    -Version "1.0.0.0"

The resulting EXE still uses the Windows LAPS PowerShell components on the workstation, so your existing prerequisite remains:

Get-LapsADPassword

must be available, and the signed-in Helpdesk technician's AD permissions still control what passwords they can retrieve. It doesn't embed elevated credentials or bypass LAPS security.

One thing I'd strongly recommend before deploying it widely: code-sign the EXE with your organization's code-signing certificate if you have one. PowerShell-to-EXE wrappers can sometimes 
attract EDR/AV scrutiny simply because malware also uses that packaging technique. A signed internal Helpdesk utility is much cleaner operationally.
